The pattern, executed:

```go
tx, err := db.Begin()
if err != nil { return err }
defer tx.Rollback()  // SAFE: no-op if Commit already succeeded

_, err = tx.Exec("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)", "Cy", "cy@x.com")
if err != nil {
    return err  // rollback happens via defer — the insert is undone
}

_, err = tx.Exec("UPDATE counters SET n = n + 1 WHERE key = 'users'")
if err != nil {
    return err  // same — everything undone
}

return tx.Commit()  // both operations atomically visible
```

Verified: after Commit — both changes present. After Rollback — neither.

The defer-Rollback idiom: tx.Rollback returns an error if the transaction is already committed — the deferred call's error is ignored (it's the safety net, not the primary path). The pattern:

```go
defer func() {
    if rerr := tx.Rollback(); rerr != nil && rerr != sql.ErrTxDone {
        log.Printf("rollback: %v", rerr)  // unexpected
    }
}()
```

The isolation (what a transaction guarantees): ACID — Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (constraints hold), Isolated (concurrent transactions don't see partial state), Durable (committed = survives crash). The isolation levels (read committed, repeatable read, serializable) tune the concurrency trade.

The nesting story: database/sql has NO nested transactions — tx on tx is a driver question (savepoints are the SQL-level emulation). The common pattern: one transaction per business operation.
